{"about":"7043957a83082eac14fe82fd2fee22d8e8267cf00f35e5ac504f3b9944aae437","body":"The claim that accepting this view collapses the distinction between corroboration and probability misunderstands the nature of corroboration. Corroboration is a report on how well a theory has survived tests so far, not a measure of its likelihood of being true. One can rationally prefer the best-tested theory for practical purposes without assigning it a higher probability value, thus maintaining Popper's core solution to the problem of induction while admitting a non-inductive pragmatic basis for action.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Conflation of corroboration with probability"}